  "subtitle": "when a strum plate makes everyone a musician",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Omnichord's 'Sonic Strings' strum plate is a genuinely novel tactile interface: a touch-sensitive strip that produces harp-like arpeggios from whatever chord button you're holding down. You cannot play a wrong note — every position on the plate maps to an in-key note for the selected chord across a 4-octave range. This is a constraint-based musical interface: the instrument limits your options to make expression accessible to non-musicians. Hold a chord button with your left hand, strum the touchplate with your right, and the Omnichord fills in the harmony, bassline, and rhythm automatically. It was designed as an electronic autoharp — a portable, fail-safe accompaniment machine for singers and music classrooms. Commercially successful from 1981 through 1996, revived in 2024, and used by everyone from Brian Eno to David Bowie to Gorillaz. Two CC-licensed images on Wikimedia Commons.",
